{
    "background_story": "Jedidiah was a young writer living in the fog-shrouded streets of Victorian London. Inspired by the works of Charles Dickens, he sought to capture the city's gritty essence in his own writing. By day, he toiled as a clerk in a dusty office, but by night, he prowled the alleys and taverns, gathering tales of the city's forgotten souls. Jedidiah's stories, filled with vivid characters and social commentary, began to gain attention from local publishers. His breakthrough novel, 'The Shadows of Whitechapel', was hailed as a masterpiece, drawing comparisons to the great Dickens himself. As Jedidiah's star rose, he remained committed to telling the stories of the marginalized and oppressed, using his words to bring hope and justice to the darkest corners of the city.",
